(; constants used by Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Basic, declared with their translated types ;)

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton__app : (a1 : Set) -> (a2 : Elem F) -> (a3 : Elem X) -> Elem F.

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
fderivWithin__app : (a1 : Set) -> (a2 : (Elem X -> F)) -> (a3 : Elem (powerset X)) -> (a4 : Elem X) -> (a5 : Elem X) -> Elem F.

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
fderiv__app : (a1 : Set) -> (a2 : (Elem X -> X)) -> (a3 : Elem X) -> (a4 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A point `x` is an accumulation point of a filter `F` if `𝓝[≠] x ⊓ F ≠ ⊥`. See also `ClusterPt`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AccPt ;)
AccPt : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (F : Elem (Filter X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: **Alias** of the reverse direction of `uniqueDiffWithinAt_iff_accPt`.  ---  In one dimension, a point is a point of unique differentiability of a set iff it is an accumulation point of the set. ;)
(; //LEAN:AccPt.uniqueDiffWithinAt ;)
AccPt__uniqueDiffWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedDivisionRing X)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (AccPt X x1 (Filter__principal X s))) -> Proof (UniqueDiffWithinAt X X s x1).

(; doc: An additive commutative group is an additive group with commutative `(+)`.  [Wikidata Q181296](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q181296) ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommGroup ;)
AddCommGroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The Landau notation `f =O[l] g` where `f` and `g` are two functions on a type `α` and `l` is a filter on `α`, means that eventually for `l`, `‖f‖` is bounded by a constant multiple of `‖g‖`. In other words, `‖f‖ / ‖g‖` is eventually bounded, modulo division by zero issues that are avoided by this definition. ;)
(; //LEAN:Asymptotics.IsBigO ;)
Asymptotics__IsBigO : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> E)) -> (g : (Elem X -> F)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Two functions `u` and `v` are said to be asymptotically equivalent along a filter `l` (denoted as `u ~[l] v` in the `Asymptotics` namespace) when `u x - v x = o(v x)` as `x` converges along `l`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Asymptotics.IsEquivalent ;)
Asymptotics__IsEquivalent : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (u : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (v : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The Landau notation `f =o[l] g` where `f` and `g` are two functions on a type `α` and `l` is a filter on `α`, means that eventually for `l`, `‖f‖` is bounded by an arbitrarily small constant multiple of `‖g‖`. In other words, `‖f‖ / ‖g‖` tends to `0` along `l`, modulo division by zero issues that are avoided by this definition. ;)
(; //LEAN:Asymptotics.IsLittleO ;)
Asymptotics__IsLittleO : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> E)) -> (g : (Elem X -> F)) -> Prop.

(; doc: We say that `f` is `Θ(g)` along a filter `l` (notation: `f =Θ[l] g`) if `f =O[l] g` and `g =O[l] f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Asymptotics.IsTheta ;)
Asymptotics__IsTheta : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> E)) -> (g : (Elem X -> F)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Set / lattice complement  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `ᶜ` in identifiers is `compl`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Compl.compl ;)
Compl__compl : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Basic hypothesis to talk about a topological additive monoid or a topological additive semigroup. A topological additive monoid over `M`, for example, is obtained by requiring both the instances `AddMonoid M` and `ContinuousAdd M`.  Continuity in each argument separately can be stated using `SeparatelyContinuousAdd α`. If one wants only continuity in either the left or right argument, but not both one can use `ContinuousConstVAdd α α`/`ContinuousConstVAdd αᵐᵒᵖ α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousAdd ;)
ContinuousAdd : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is continuous at a point `x₀` if `f x` tends to `f x₀` when `x` tends to `x₀`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousAt ;)
ContinuousAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:ContinuousAt.continuousWithinAt ;)
ContinuousAt__continuousWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Y)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (ContinuousAt X Y f x2)) -> Proof (ContinuousWithinAt X Y f s x2).

(; doc: Continuous linear maps between modules. We only put the type classes that are necessary for the definition, although in applications `M` and `M₂` will be topological modules over the topological ring `R`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap ;)
ContinuousLinearMap : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: the identity map as a continuous linear map. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.id ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__id : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X1 X1).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.mk ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__mk : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (toLinearMap : Elem (LinearMap R S x2 M X)) -> (cont : Proof (autoParam (Continuous M X (AddHom__toFun M X (LinearMap__toAddHom R S x2 M X toLinearMap))) ContinuousLinearMap__cont___autoParam)) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap R S x2 M X).

(; doc: Given an element `x` of a topological space `M` over a semiring `R`, the natural continuous linear map from `R` to `M` by taking multiples of `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.toSpanSingleton ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x5 : Elem X1) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.toSpanSingleton.congr_simp ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton__congr_simp : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X1)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X1)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (x5 : Elem X1) -> (x6 : Elem X1) -> (e_x : Proof (Eqt X1 x5 x6)) -> Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1) (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X X1 x5) (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X X1 x6)).

(; //LEAN:ContinuousLinearMap.toSpanSingleton_inj ;)
ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton_inj : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X1)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X1)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (f : Elem X1) -> (f' : Elem X1) -> Proof (iff (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) X X1) (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X X1 f) (ContinuousLinearMap__toSpanSingleton X X1 f')) (Eqt X1 f f')).

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is continuous on a subset `s` when it's continuous at every point of `s` within `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousOn ;)
ContinuousOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function between topological spaces is continuous at a point `x₀` within a subset `s` if `f x` tends to `f x₀` when `x` tends to `x₀` while staying within `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ContinuousWithinAt ;)
ContinuousWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: The coercion from `F` to a function. ;)
(; //LEAN:DFunLike.coe ;)
DFunLike__coe : (F : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (x1 : Elem F) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem (x a).

(; doc: A function `f` is differentiable at a point `x` if it admits a derivative there (possibly non-unique). ;)
(; //LEAN:DifferentiableAt ;)
DifferentiableAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableAt.hasFDerivAt ;)
DifferentiableAt__hasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableAt X E F f x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f (fderiv X E F f x7) x7).

(; doc: `DifferentiableOn 𝕜 f s` means that `f` is differentiable within `s` at any point of `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DifferentiableOn ;)
DifferentiableOn : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableOn.hasFDerivAt ;)
DifferentiableOn__hasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableOn X E F f s)) -> (hs : Proof (Membership__mem (powerset E) (Filter E) (nhds E x7) s)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f (fderiv X E F f x7) x7).

(; doc: A function `f` is differentiable at a point `x` within a set `s` if it admits a derivative there (possibly non-unique). ;)
(; //LEAN:DifferentiableWithinAt ;)
DifferentiableWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:DifferentiableWithinAt.hasFDerivWithinAt ;)
DifferentiableWithinAt__hasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (DifferentiableWithinAt X E F f s x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f (fderivWithin X E F f s x7) s x7).

(; doc: The equality relation. It has one introduction rule, `Eq.refl`. We use `a = b` as notation for `Eq a b`. A fundamental property of equality is that it is an equivalence relation. ``` variable (α : Type) (a b c d : α) variable (hab : a = b) (hcb : c = b) (hcd : c = d)  example : a = d :=   Eq.trans (Eq.trans hab (Eq.symm hcb)) hcd ``` Equality is much more than an equivalence relation, however. It has the important property that every assertion respects the equivalence, in the sense that we can substitute equal expressions without changing the truth value. That is, given `h1 : a = b` and `h2 : p a`, we can construct a proof for `p b` using substitution: `Eq.subst h1 h2`. Example: ``` example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)         (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   Eq.subst h1 h2  example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)     (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   h1 ▸ h2 ``` The triangle in the second presentation is a macro built on top of `Eq.subst` and `Eq.symm`, and you can enter it by typing `\t`. For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `=` in identifiers is `eq`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq ;)
Eq : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: A filter `F` on a type `α` is a collection of sets of `α` which contains the whole `α`, is upwards-closed, and is stable under intersection. We do not forbid this collection to be all sets of `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter ;)
Filter : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `f.Eventually p` or `∀ᶠ x in f, p x` mean that `{x | p x} ∈ f`. E.g., `∀ᶠ x in atTop, p x` means that `p` holds true for sufficiently large `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.Eventually ;)
Filter__Eventually : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (f : Elem (Filter X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Filter.Eventually.mono ;)
Filter__Eventually__mono : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (f : Elem (Filter X)) -> (hp : Proof (Filter__Eventually X (x2 => p x2) f)) -> (hq : Proof (forall X (x1 => if (p x1) (q x1)))) -> Proof (Filter__Eventually X (x => q x) f).

(; doc: If a predicate is true in a neighborhood of `x`, then it is true for `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.Eventually.self_of_nhds ;)
Filter__Eventually__self_of_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (h : Proof (Filter__Eventually X (y => p y) (nhds X x1))) -> Proof (p x1).

(; //LEAN:Filter.Eventually.self_of_nhdsWithin ;)
Filter__Eventually__self_of_nhdsWithin : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Filter__Eventually X (y => p y) (nhdsWithin X x1 s))) -> (hx : Proof (Membership__mem X (powerset X) s x1)) -> Proof (p x1).

(; doc: Two functions `f` and `g` are *eventually equal* along a filter `l` if the set of `x` such that `f x = g x` belongs to `l`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.eq_of_nhdsWithin ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__eq_of_nhdsWithin : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X Y (nhdsWithin X a s) f g)) -> (hmem : Proof (Membership__mem X (powerset X) s a)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a) (g a)).

(; doc: If two functions are equal in a neighbourhood of `x`, then for `y` sufficiently close to `x` these functions are equal in a neighbourhood of `y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.eventuallyEq_nhds ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__eventuallyEq_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> X1)) -> (g : (Elem X -> X1)) -> (h : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X X1 (nhds X x1) f g)) -> Proof (Filter__Eventually X (y => Filter__EventuallyEq X X1 (nhds X y) f g) (nhds X x1)).

(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.filter_mono ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__filter_mono : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (l' : Elem (Filter X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X Y l f g)) -> (h1 : Proof (Leq l' l)) -> Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X Y l' f g).

(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.hasFDerivAtFilter_iff ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__hasFDerivAtFilter_iff : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (x7 : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x8 : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x9 : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x10 : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> (h : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq (Prod E E) (Prod F F) L (Prod__map E F E F x7 x7) (Prod__map E F E F x8 x8))) -> (h1 : Proof (forall E (x11 => Eqt F (x9 x11) (x10 x11)))) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F x7 x9 L) (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F x8 x10 L)).

(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.prodMap ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__prodMap : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (W : Set) -> (la : Elem (Filter X)) -> (fa : (Elem X -> Z)) -> (ga : (Elem X -> Z)) -> (ha : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X Z la fa ga)) -> (lb : Elem (Filter Y)) -> (fb : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (gb : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (hb : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq Y W lb fb gb)) -> Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq (Prod X Y) (Prod Z W) (SProd__sprod (Filter X) (Filter Y) (Filter (Prod X Y)) la lb) (Prod__map X Z Y W fa fb) (Prod__map X Z Y W ga gb)).

(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.prodMap_nhds ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__prodMap_nhds : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace Y)) -> (X1 : Set) -> (Y1 : Set) -> (x2 : (Elem X -> X1)) -> (x3 : (Elem X -> X1)) -> (x4 : (Elem Y -> Y1)) -> (x5 : (Elem Y -> Y1)) -> (x6 : Elem X) -> (y : Elem Y) -> (hf : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X X1 (nhds X x6) x2 x3)) -> (hg : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq Y Y1 (nhds Y y) x4 x5)) -> Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq (Prod X Y) (Prod X1 Y1) (nhds (Prod X Y) (Prod__mk X Y x6 y)) (Prod__map X X1 Y Y1 x2 x4) (Prod__map X X1 Y Y1 x3 x5)).

(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEq.symm ;)
Filter__EventuallyEq__symm : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (H : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X Y l f g)) -> Proof (Filter__EventuallyEq X Y l g f).

(; doc: Two sets `s` and `t` are *eventually equal* along a filter `l` if the set of `x` such that `x ∈ s ↔ x ∈ t` belongs to `l`.  This is definitionally `(· ∈ s) =ᶠ[l] (· ∈ t)`, but it is a separate definition (rather than an abbreviation) to avoid simp unfolding membership of concrete sets. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.EventuallyEqSet ;)
Filter__EventuallyEqSet : (X : Set) -> (l : Elem (Filter X)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (t : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Filter.Tendsto` is the generic "limit of a function" predicate. `Tendsto f l₁ l₂` asserts that for every `l₂` neighborhood `a`, the `f`-preimage of `a` is an `l₁` neighborhood. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.Tendsto ;)
Filter__Tendsto : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem (Filter X)) -> (x1 : Elem (Filter Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: In any topological space, the open sets with discrete complement form a filter, defined as the supremum of all punctured neighborhoods.  See `Filter.mem_codiscrete'` for the equivalence. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.codiscrete ;)
Filter__codiscrete : (X : Set) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; doc: The filter of sets with no accumulation points inside a set `S : Set X`, implemented as the supremum over all punctured neighborhoods within `S`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.codiscreteWithin ;)
Filter__codiscreteWithin : (X : Set) -> (S : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; doc: The principal filter of `s` is the collection of all supersets of `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Filter.principal ;)
Filter__principal : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; doc: Function composition, usually written with the infix operator `∘`. A new function is created from two existing functions, where one function's output is used as input to the other.  Examples:  * `Function.comp List.reverse (List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  * `(List.reverse ∘ List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∘` in identifiers is `comp`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.comp ;)
Function__comp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (W : Set) -> (f : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem W.

(; doc: The constant function that ignores its argument.  If `a : α`, then `Function.const β a : β → α` is the “constant function with value `a`”. For all arguments `b : β`, `Function.const β a b = a`. It is often written directly as `fun _ => a`.  Examples:  * `Function.const Bool 10 true = 10`  * `Function.const Bool 10 false = 10`  * `Function.const String 10 "any string" = 10` ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.const ;)
Function__const : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (x : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `a • b` computes the product of `a` and `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent, but it is intended to be used for left actions.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `•` in identifiers is `smul`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HSMul.hSMul ;)
HSMul__hSMul : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem Y) -> Elem Z.

(; doc: A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative at `x` if `f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` tends to `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt ;)
HasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.congr_fderiv ;)
HasFDerivAt__congr_fderiv : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (g' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> (h' : Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F) f' g')) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f g' x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.differentiableAt ;)
HasFDerivAt__differentiableAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> Proof (DifferentiableAt X E F f x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.hasFDerivAtFilter ;)
HasFDerivAt__hasFDerivAtFilter : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> (hL : Proof (Leq L (SProd__sprod (Filter E) (Filter E) (Filter (Prod E E)) (nhds E x7) (Pure__pure Filter E x7)))) -> Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' L).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.hasFDerivWithinAt ;)
HasFDerivAt__hasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.isBigO_sub ;)
HasFDerivAt__isBigO_sub : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x5)) -> Proof (Asymptotics__IsBigO E F E (nhds E x5) (x6 => minus (f x6) (f x5)) (x7 => minus x7 x5)).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAt.unique ;)
HasFDerivAt__unique : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x8 : Elem E) -> (_ : Proof (T2Space F)) -> (h5 : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x8)) -> (h6 : Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f x7 x8)) -> Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F) f' x7).

(; doc: A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative along the filter `L` if `f x₁ = f x₂ + f' (x₁ - x₂) + o (x₁ - x₂)` when `x = (x₁, x₂)` converges along the filter `L`. This definition is designed to be specialized  - for `L = 𝓝 (x, x)` (in `HasStrictFDerivAt`),   giving rise to the derivative in the sense of strict differentiability; - for `L = 𝓝 x ×ˢ pure x` (in `HasFDerivAt`), giving rise to the usual notion of Fréchet derivative; - for `L = 𝓝[s] x ×ˢ pure x` (in `HasFDerivWithinAt`),   giving rise to the notion of Fréchet derivative along the set `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAtFilter ;)
HasFDerivAtFilter : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAtFilter.isBigO_sub ;)
HasFDerivAtFilter__isBigO_sub : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' L)) -> Proof (Asymptotics__IsBigO (Prod E E) F E L (p => minus (f (Prod__fst E E p)) (f (Prod__snd E E p))) (p1 => minus (Prod__fst E E p1) (Prod__snd E E p1))).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAtFilter.mono ;)
HasFDerivAtFilter__mono : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> (x8 : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' x8)) -> (hst : Proof (Leq x7 x8)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivAtFilter.tendsto_nhds ;)
HasFDerivAtFilter__tendsto_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd E)) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd F)) -> (L : Elem (Filter E)) -> (hL : Proof (Leq L (nhds E x7))) -> (h4 : Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' (SProd__sprod (Filter E) (Filter E) (Filter (Prod E E)) L (Pure__pure Filter E x7)))) -> Proof (Filter__Tendsto E F f L (nhds F (f x7))).

(; doc: A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative at `x` within a set `s` if `f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` tends to `x` inside `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.congr_fderiv ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__congr_fderiv : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (g' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)) -> (h' : Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F) f' g')) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f g' s x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.congr_mono ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__congr_mono : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x8 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x8)) -> (ht : Proof (Set__EqOn E F x7 f t)) -> (hx : Proof (Eqt F (x7 x8) (f x8))) -> (h1 : Proof (Set__Subset E t s)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F x7 f' t x8).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.differentiableWithinAt ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__differentiableWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)) -> Proof (DifferentiableWithinAt X E F f s x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.hasFDerivAt ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__hasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)) -> (hs : Proof (Membership__mem (powerset E) (Filter E) (nhds E x7) s)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.mono ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__mono : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' t x7)) -> (hst : Proof (Set__Subset E s t)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.mono_of_mem_nhdsWithin ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__mono_of_mem_nhdsWithin : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' t x7)) -> (hst : Proof (Membership__mem (powerset E) (Filter E) (nhdsWithin E x7 s) t)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7).

(; //LEAN:HasFDerivWithinAt.union ;)
HasFDerivWithinAt__union : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (hs : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)) -> (ht : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' t x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' (Union__union (powerset E) s t) x7).

(; doc: A function `f` has derivative `f'` at `a` in the sense of *strict differentiability* if `f x - f y - f' (x - y) = o(x - y)` as `x, y → a`. This form of differentiability is required, e.g., by the inverse function theorem. Any `C^1` function on a vector space over `ℝ` is strictly differentiable but this definition works, e.g., for vector spaces over `p`-adic numbers. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasStrictFDerivAt ;)
HasStrictFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasStrictFDerivAt.congr_fderiv ;)
HasStrictFDerivAt__congr_fderiv : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (g' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (h : Proof (HasStrictFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> (h' : Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F) f' g')) -> Proof (HasStrictFDerivAt X E F f g' x7).

(; //LEAN:HasStrictFDerivAt.hasFDerivAt ;)
HasStrictFDerivAt__hasFDerivAt : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (hf : Proof (HasStrictFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)) -> Proof (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7).

(; //LEAN:Iff.rfl ;)
Iff__rfl : (a : Prop) -> Proof (iff a a).

(; //LEAN:Iio_mem_nhds ;)
Iio_mem_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (LinearOrder X)) -> (_ : Proof (ClosedIciTopology X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h1 : Proof (Lt b a)) -> Proof (Membership__mem (powerset X) (Filter X) (nhds X b) (Set__Iio X a)).

(; doc: The canonical homomorphism `Int → R`. In most use cases, the target type will have a ring structure, and this homomorphism should be a ring homomorphism.  `IntCast` and `NatCast` exist to allow different libraries with their own types that can be notated as natural numbers to have consistent `simp` normal forms without needing to create coercion simplification sets that are aware of all combinations. Libraries should make it easy to work with `IntCast` where possible. For instance, in Mathlib there will be such a homomorphism (and thus an `IntCast R` instance) whenever `R` is an additive group with a `1`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Int.cast ;)
Int__cast : (R : Set) -> (x1 : Elem Int) -> Elem R.

(; doc: `a ∩ b` is the intersection of `a` and `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∩` in identifiers is `inter`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inter.inter ;)
Inter__inter : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `a⁻¹` computes the inverse of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⁻¹` in identifiers is `inv`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inv.inv ;)
Inv__inv : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `IsOpen s` means that `s` is open in the ambient topological space on `X` ;)
(; //LEAN:IsOpen ;)
IsOpen : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:IsOpen.mem_nhds ;)
IsOpen__mem_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (hs : Proof (IsOpen X s)) -> (hx : Proof (Membership__mem X (powerset X) s x1)) -> Proof (Membership__mem (powerset X) (Filter X) (nhds X x1) s).

(; //LEAN:IsOpen.uniqueDiffWithinAt ;)
IsOpen__uniqueDiffWithinAt : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivisionSemiring X)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (Filter__NeBot X (nhdsWithin X 0 (Compl__compl (powerset X) (Singleton__singleton X (powerset X) 0))))) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd E)) -> (hs : Proof (IsOpen E s)) -> (xs : Proof (Membership__mem E (powerset E) s x5)) -> Proof (UniqueDiffWithinAt X E s x5).

(; doc: An isometry is continuous. ;)
(; //LEAN:Isometry.continuous ;)
Isometry__continuous : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (PseudoEMetricSpace X)) -> (_ : Proof (PseudoEMetricSpace Y)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (hf : Proof (Isometry X Y f)) -> Proof (Continuous X Y f).

(; doc: A linear order is reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric and total relation `≤`. We assume that every linear ordered type has decidable `(≤)`, `(<)`, and `(=)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearOrder ;)
LinearOrder : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` is **Lipschitz continuous** with constant `K ≥ 0` **on `s`** if for all `x, y` in `s` we have `dist (f x) (f y) ≤ K * dist x y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LipschitzOnWith ;)
LipschitzOnWith : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (K : Elem NNReal) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` is **Lipschitz continuous** with constant `K ≥ 0` if for all `x, y` we have `dist (f x) (f y) ≤ K * dist x y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LipschitzWith ;)
LipschitzWith : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (K : Elem NNReal) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The membership relation `a ∈ s : Prop` where `a : α`, `s : γ`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∈` in identifiers is `mem`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Membership.mem ;)
Membership__mem : (X : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem Z) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: A module is a generalization of vector spaces to a scalar semiring. It consists of a scalar semiring `R` and an additive monoid of "vectors" `M`, connected by a "scalar multiplication" operation `r • x : M` (where `r : R` and `x : M`) with some natural associativity and distributivity axioms similar to those on a ring. ;)
(; //LEAN:Module ;)
Module : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Nonnegative real numbers, denoted as `ℝ≥0` within the NNReal namespace ;)
(; //LEAN:NNReal ;)
NNReal : Set.

(; doc: Coercion `ℝ≥0 → ℝ`. ;)
(; //LEAN:NNReal.toReal ;)
NNReal__toReal : (x : Elem NNReal) -> Elem Real.

(; doc: The canonical homomorphism `Nat → R`. In most use cases, the target type will have a (semi)ring structure, and this homomorphism should be a (semi)ring homomorphism.  `NatCast` and `IntCast` exist to allow different libraries with their own types that can be notated as natural numbers to have consistent `simp` normal forms without needing to create coercion simplification sets that are aware of all combinations. Libraries should make it easy to work with `NatCast` where possible. For instance, in Mathlib there will be such a homomorphism (and thus a `NatCast R` instance) whenever `R` is an additive monoid with a `1`.  The prototypical example is `Int.ofNat`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.cast ;)
Nat__cast : (R : Set) -> (x1 : Elem Nat) -> Elem R.

(; //LEAN:NegZeroClass.toZero ;)
NegZeroClass__toZero : (G : Set) -> Proof (Zero G).

(; doc: A nontrivially normed field is a normed field in which there is an element of norm different from `0` and `1`. This makes it possible to bring any element arbitrarily close to `0` by multiplication by the powers of any element, and thus to relate algebra and topology. ;)
(; //LEAN:NontriviallyNormedField ;)
NontriviallyNormedField : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: the `ℝ`-valued norm function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Norm.norm ;)
Norm__norm : (E : Set) -> (x : Elem E) -> Elem Real.

(; doc: A normed group is an additive group endowed with a norm for which `dist x y = ‖-x + y‖` defines a metric space structure. ;)
(; //LEAN:NormedAddCommGroup ;)
NormedAddCommGroup : (E : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A normed space over a normed field is a vector space endowed with a norm which satisfies the equality `‖c • x‖ = ‖c‖ ‖x‖`. We require only `‖c • x‖ ≤ ‖c‖ ‖x‖` in the definition, then prove `‖c • x‖ = ‖c‖ ‖x‖` in `norm_smul`.  Note that since this requires `SeminormedAddCommGroup` and not `NormedAddCommGroup`, this typeclass can be used for "seminormed spaces" too, just as `Module` can be used for "semimodules". ;)
(; //LEAN:NormedSpace ;)
NormedSpace : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The class `OfNat α n` powers the numeric literal parser. If you write `37 : α`, Lean will attempt to synthesize `OfNat α 37`, and will generate the term `(OfNat.ofNat 37 : α)`.  There is a bit of infinite regress here since the desugaring apparently still contains a literal `37` in it. The type of expressions contains a primitive constructor for "raw natural number literals", which you can directly access using the macro `nat_lit 37`. Raw number literals are always of type `Nat`. So it would be more correct to say that Lean looks for an instance of `OfNat α (nat_lit 37)`, and it generates the term `(OfNat.ofNat (nat_lit 37) : α)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:OfNat ;)
OfNat : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> Prop.

(; doc: The `OfNat.ofNat` function is automatically inserted by the parser when the user writes a numeric literal like `1 : α`. Implementations of this typeclass can therefore customize the behavior of `n : α` based on `n` and `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:OfNat.ofNat ;)
OfNat__ofNat : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A topology on a set which is both a topological space and a preorder is _order-closed_ if the set of points `(x, y)` with `x ≤ y` is closed in the product space. We introduce this as a mixin. This property is satisfied for the order topology on a linear order, but it can be satisfied more generally, and suffices to derive many interesting properties relating order and topology. ;)
(; //LEAN:OrderClosedTopology ;)
OrderClosedTopology : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A partial order is a reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric relation `≤`. ;)
(; //LEAN:PartialOrder ;)
PartialOrder : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The product type, usually written `α × β`. Product types are also called pair or tuple types. Elements of this type are pairs in which the first element is an `α` and the second element is a `β`.  Products nest to the right, so `(x, y, z) : α × β × γ` is equivalent to `(x, (y, z)) : α × (β × γ)`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `×` in identifiers is `Prod`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod ;)
Prod : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The first element of a pair. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.fst ;)
Prod__fst : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (self : Elem (Prod X Y)) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Transforms a pair by applying functions to both elements.  Examples: * `(1, 2).map (· + 1) (· * 3) = (2, 6)` * `(1, 2).map toString (· * 3) = ("1", 6)` ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.map ;)
Prod__map : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (X2 : Set) -> (X3 : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X1)) -> (g : (Elem X2 -> X3)) -> (x : Elem (Prod X X2)) -> Elem (Prod X1 X3).

(; doc: Constructs a pair. This is usually written `(x, y)` instead of `Prod.mk x y`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `(a, b)` in identifiers is `mk`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.mk ;)
Prod__mk : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (fst : Elem X) -> (snd : Elem Y) -> Elem (Prod X Y).

(; doc: The second element of a pair. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.snd ;)
Prod__snd : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (self : Elem (Prod X Y)) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: Given `a : α`, then `pure a : f α` represents an action that does nothing and returns `a`.  Examples: * `(pure "hello" : Option String) = some "hello"` * `(pure "hello" : Except (Array String) String) = Except.ok "hello"` * `(pure "hello" : StateM Nat String).run 105 = ("hello", 105)` ;)
(; //LEAN:Pure.pure ;)
Pure__pure : (f : (Set -> Set)) -> (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem (f X).

(; doc: Bundled semiring homomorphisms; use this for bundled ring homomorphisms too.  This extends from both `MonoidHom` and `MonoidWithZeroHom` in order to put the fields in a sensible order, even though `MonoidWithZeroHom` already extends `MonoidHom`. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom ;)
RingHom : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The identity ring homomorphism from a semiring to itself. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom.id ;)
RingHom__id : (X : Set) -> Elem (RingHom X X).

(; //LEAN:RingHom.isometry ;)
RingHom__isometry : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedRing X)) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedRing X1)) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom X X1)) -> (_ : Proof (RingHomIsometric X X1 x2)) -> Proof (Isometry X X1 x2).

(; doc: Interpret a `RingHom` `f` as an `f`-semilinear map. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom.toSemilinearMap ;)
RingHom__toSemilinearMap : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (f : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> Elem (LinearMap R S f R S).

(; doc: Class that expresses the fact that two ring homomorphisms are inverses of each other. This is used to handle `symm` for semilinear equivalences. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHomInvPair ;)
RingHomInvPair : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom X X1)) -> (x3 : Elem (RingHom X1 X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: This class states that a ring homomorphism is isometric. This is a sufficient assumption for a continuous semilinear map to be bounded and this is the main use for this typeclass. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHomIsometric ;)
RingHomIsometric : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x4 : Elem (RingHom X X1)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `a \ b` is the set difference of `a` and `b`, consisting of all elements in `a` that are not in `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `\` in identifiers is `sdiff`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SDiff.sdiff ;)
SDiff__sdiff : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The Cartesian product `s ×ˢ t` is the set of `(a, b)` such that `a ∈ s` and `b ∈ t`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SProd.sprod ;)
SProd__sprod : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem Y) -> Elem Z.

(; doc: Two functions `f₁ f₂ : α → β` are equal on `s` if `f₁ x = f₂ x` for all `x ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.EqOn ;)
Set__EqOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x1 : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Ici a` is the left-closed right-infinite interval $[a, ∞)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ici ;)
Set__Ici : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Iic b` is the left-infinite right-closed interval $(-∞, b]$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Iic ;)
Set__Iic : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Iio b` is the left-infinite right-open interval $(-∞, b)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Iio ;)
Set__Iio : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Ioi a` is the left-open right-infinite interval $(a, ∞)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ioi ;)
Set__Ioi : (X : Set) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `Ioo a b` is the left-open right-open interval $(a, b)$. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Ioo ;)
Set__Ioo : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: The subset relation on sets. `s ⊆ t` means that all elements of `s` are elements of `t`.  Note that you should **not** use this definition directly, but instead write `s ⊆ t`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Subset ;)
Set__Subset : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x1 : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Set.Subset.refl ;)
Set__Subset__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset X a a).

(; //LEAN:Set.Subset.rfl ;)
Set__Subset__rfl : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset X s s).

(; //LEAN:Set.subset_univ ;)
Set__subset_univ : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset X s (Set__univ X)).

(; doc: The universal set on a type `α` is the set containing all elements of `α`.  This is conceptually the "same as" `α` (in set theory, it is actually the same), but type theory makes the distinction that `α` is a type while `Set.univ` is a term of type `Set α`. `Set.univ` can itself be coerced to a type `↥Set.univ` which is in bijection with (but distinct from) `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.univ ;)
Set__univ : (X : Set) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: `singleton x` is a collection with the single element `x` (notation: `{x}`).  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{x}` in identifiers is `singleton`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Singleton.singleton ;)
Singleton__singleton : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: A T₂ space, also known as a Hausdorff space, is one in which for every `x ≠ y` there exists disjoint open sets around `x` and `y`. This is the most widely used of the separation axioms. ;)
(; //LEAN:T2Space ;)
T2Space : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A topology on `X`. ;)
(; //LEAN:TopologicalSpace ;)
TopologicalSpace : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: `a ∪ b` is the union of `a` and `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∪` in identifiers is `union`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Union.union ;)
Union__union : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at `x` spans a dense subset of the whole space. The main role of this property is to ensure that the differential within `s` at `x` is unique, hence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `UniqueDiffWithinAt.eq` in `Mathlib/Analysis/Calculus/FDeriv/Basic.lean`. To avoid pathologies in dimension 0, we also require that `x` belongs to the closure of `s` (which is automatic when `E` is not `0`-dimensional). ;)
(; //LEAN:UniqueDiffWithinAt ;)
UniqueDiffWithinAt : (R : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x4 : Elem E) -> Prop.

(; doc: `UniqueDiffWithinAt` achieves its goal: it implies the uniqueness of the derivative. ;)
(; //LEAN:UniqueDiffWithinAt.eq ;)
UniqueDiffWithinAt__eq : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (_ : Proof (ContinuousAdd F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x8 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (_ : Proof (T2Space F)) -> (H : Proof (UniqueDiffWithinAt X E s x8)) -> (hf : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x8)) -> (hg : Proof (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f x7 s x8)) -> Proof (Eqt (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F) f' x7).

(; doc: The closure of `s` is the smallest closed set containing `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:closure ;)
closure : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: Similar to `congrFun` but `β` does not depend on `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congrFun' ;)
congrFun' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) f g)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a) (g a)).

(; doc: **Alias** of `congrArg`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congr_arg ;)
congr_arg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; //LEAN:em' ;)
em' : (p : Prop) -> Proof (or (not p) p).

(; //LEAN:eq_self ;)
eq_self : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Eqt X a a) True).

(; doc: If `f` has a derivative at `x`, then `fderiv 𝕜 f x` is such a derivative. Otherwise, it is set to `0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:fderiv ;)
fderiv : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F).

(; doc: If `f` has a derivative at `x` within `s`, then `fderivWithin 𝕜 f s x` is such a derivative. Otherwise, it is set to `0`. We also set it to be zero, if zero is one of possible derivatives. ;)
(; //LEAN:fderivWithin ;)
fderivWithin : (X : Set) -> (E : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F).

(; //LEAN:forall_congr ;)
forall_congr : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (a2 => Eqt Prop (p a2) (q a2)))) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (forall X (a => p a)) (forall X (a1 => q a1))).

(; doc: **Function extensionality.** If two functions return equal results for all possible arguments, then they are equal.  It is called “extensionality” because it provides a way to prove two objects equal based on the properties of the underlying mathematical functions, rather than based on the syntax used to denote them. Function extensionality is a theorem that can be [proved using quotient types](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=quotient-funext). ;)
(; //LEAN:funext ;)
funext : (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (f : ((x4 : Elem X) -> x x4)) -> (g : ((x3 : Elem X) -> x x3)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (x2 => Eqt (x x2) (f x2) (g x2)))) -> Proof (Eqt ((x1 : Elem X) -> x x1) f g).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAtFilter_const ;)
hasFDerivAtFilter_const : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (c : Elem F) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F (x7 => c) 0 L).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAtFilter_id ;)
hasFDerivAtFilter_id : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> Proof (HasFDerivAtFilter X E E (id E) (ContinuousLinearMap__id X E) L).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAtFilter_iff_isLittleO ;)
hasFDerivAtFilter_iff_isLittleO : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' L) (Asymptotics__IsLittleO (Prod E E) F E L (p => minus (minus (f (Prod__fst E E p)) (f (Prod__snd E E p))) (f' (minus (Prod__fst E E p) (Prod__snd E E p)))) (p1 => minus (Prod__fst E E p1) (Prod__snd E E p1)))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAtFilter_iff_tendsto ;)
hasFDerivAtFilter_iff_tendsto : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (L : Elem (Filter (Prod E E))) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivAtFilter X E F f f' L) (Filter__Tendsto (Prod E E) Real (p => times (Inv__inv Real (Norm__norm E (minus (Prod__fst E E p) (Prod__snd E E p)))) (Norm__norm F (minus (minus (f (Prod__fst E E p)) (f (Prod__snd E E p))) (f' (minus (Prod__fst E E p) (Prod__snd E E p)))))) L (nhds Real 0))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAt_iff_isLittleO ;)
hasFDerivAt_iff_isLittleO : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x5) (Asymptotics__IsLittleO E F E (nhds E x5) (x' => minus (minus (f x') (f x5)) (f' (minus x' x5))) (x'1 => minus x'1 x5))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAt_iff_isLittleO_nhds_zero ;)
hasFDerivAt_iff_isLittleO_nhds_zero : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x5) (Asymptotics__IsLittleO E F E (nhds E 0) (h => minus (minus (f (plus x5 h)) (f x5)) (f' h)) (h1 => h1))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivAt_iff_tendsto ;)
hasFDerivAt_iff_tendsto : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x5) (Filter__Tendsto E Real (x' => times (Inv__inv Real (Norm__norm E (minus x' x5))) (Norm__norm F (minus (minus (f x') (f x5)) (f' (minus x' x5))))) (nhds E x5) (nhds Real 0))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_congr_set ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_congr_set : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEqSet E (nhds E x7) s t)) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7) (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' t x7)).

(; doc: In the case `y = x`, see also `hasFDerivWithinAt_congr_set_nhdsNE`, which does not require the domain to be a T₁ space. ;)
(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_congr_set' ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_congr_set' : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (_ : Proof (T1Space E)) -> (y : Elem E) -> (h1 : Proof (Filter__EventuallyEqSet E (nhdsWithin E x7 (Compl__compl (powerset E) (Singleton__singleton E (powerset E) y))) s t)) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7) (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' t x7)).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_iff_isLittleO ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_iff_isLittleO : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SeminormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x5) (Asymptotics__IsLittleO E F E (nhdsWithin E x5 s) (x' => minus (minus (f x') (f x5)) (f' (minus x' x5))) (x'1 => minus x'1 x5))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_iff_tendsto ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_iff_tendsto : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NormedAddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (NormedSpace X F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x5 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x5) (Filter__Tendsto E Real (x' => times (Inv__inv Real (Norm__norm E (minus x' x5))) (Norm__norm F (minus (minus (f x') (f x5)) (f' (minus x' x5))))) (nhdsWithin E x5 s) (nhds Real 0))).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_inter ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_inter : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (Membership__mem (powerset E) (Filter E) (nhds E x7) t)) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' (Inter__inter (powerset E) s t) x7) (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_inter' ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_inter' : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (t : Elem (powerset E)) -> (h : Proof (Membership__mem (powerset E) (Filter E) (nhdsWithin E x7 s) t)) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' (Inter__inter (powerset E) s t) x7) (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_sdiff_singleton ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_sdiff_singleton : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> (s : Elem (powerset E)) -> (_ : Proof (T1Space E)) -> (y : Elem E) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' (SDiff__sdiff (powerset E) s (Singleton__singleton E (powerset E) y)) x7) (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' s x7)).

(; //LEAN:hasFDerivWithinAt_univ ;)
hasFDerivWithinAt_univ : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NontriviallyNormedField X)) -> (E : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup E)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X E)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace E)) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup F)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X F)) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace F)) -> (f : (Elem E -> F)) -> (f' : Elem (ContinuousLinearMap X X (RingHom__id X) E F)) -> (x7 : Elem E) -> Proof (iff (HasFDerivWithinAt X E F f f' (Set__univ E) x7) (HasFDerivAt X E F f f' x7)).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:implies_true ;)
implies_true : (X : Set) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (forall X (x => True)) True).

(; //LEAN:inf_le_left ;)
inf_le_left : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SemilatticeInf X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (Leq (Min__min X a b) a).

(; //LEAN:isOpen_univ ;)
isOpen_univ : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> Proof (IsOpen X (Set__univ X)).

(; doc: A version of `le_refl` where the argument is implicit ;)
(; //LEAN:le_rfl ;)
le_rfl : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Leq a a).

(; //LEAN:mt ;)
mt : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (if a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (not b)) -> Proof (not a).

(; doc: A set is called a neighborhood of `x` if it contains an open set around `x`. The set of all neighborhoods of `x` forms a filter, the neighborhood filter at `x`, is here defined as the infimum over the principal filters of all open sets containing `x`.  [Wikidata Q3275652](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3275652) ;)
(; //LEAN:nhds ;)
nhds : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; doc: The "neighborhood within" filter. Elements of `𝓝[s] x` are sets containing the intersection of `s` and a neighborhood of `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:nhdsWithin ;)
nhdsWithin : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (Filter X).

(; //LEAN:nhdsWithin_le_nhds ;)
nhdsWithin_le_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Leq (nhdsWithin X a s) (nhds X a)).

(; //LEAN:not_imp_comm ;)
not_imp_comm : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> Proof (iff (if (not a) b) (if (not b) a)).

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; //LEAN:pure_le_nhds ;)
pure_le_nhds : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (TopologicalSpace X)) -> Proof (Leq (Pure__pure Filter X) (nhds X)).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

